Transcript corrupts under BIT_GC=stress against a Bit-sourced libbitrt.a.

The Linux twin of ../rootiodarwin/stress-waived, and NOT written from that
symmetry. That file's last paragraph said this program "drives the same
`ioProps` and will fail the same way, but it is linux-only (unbuildable on this
host) so it has NOT been measured and is deliberately NOT waived here. A waiver
without evidence is a silent loss of coverage; let the Linux gate confirm it
rather than assuming it."

The Linux gate has now confirmed it, and it produced the SECOND of the two
mismatch shapes that file predicted, verbatim. `scripts/arm64gate.sh` on
aarch64-linux:

    stress 'rootiolinux' [BIT_GC=stress]: output mismatch
      expected   P7m[panic: type assertion failed: Circle is not Square
      got        P7m[panic: type assertion failed: Square is not Square

A swept Circle's memory came back as a Square. Every other line of the
transcript matched, which is what identifies the cause as the string bytes
rather than the assertion logic: the type test itself did the right thing and
the name it printed had been collected out from under it.

The cause is ../rootio/props' `ioProps`, shared by all three programs. It holds
every port object as a raw `int` — `mine` returns an ABI.md §2 header address,
and nothing is reachable from a reference-typed slot — so once the program runs
on the live heap, any allocation can collect a string the transcript has not
written yet. There is nowhere to root them: every port object in that module is
an `int`, including the `rtSliceNew` handle inside `mine`, and that is
deliberate, because the module tests the object model BELOW the type system.

The default-policy run still asserts the whole transcript byte for byte, and
that is the run this program was written for: it checks that a provider puts the
right bytes on the right file descriptor, not that the collector traces them.

ON #1845, WHICH THE OTHER TWO WAIVERS STILL POINT AT. Both say "Tracked as
#1845, which must remove this file when it closes." #1845 is CLOSED and these
files must stay. It fixed the deterministic `bridge=0/31` and the intermittent
SIGSEGV — an unguarded `rootInit` whose `rootBindMemory` had already repointed
the live collector's heap — and its own scope note says so: "The waiver covers
ONLY the stress-policy mask. The deterministic 0/31 and the intermittent SIGSEGV
are fixed in code, not waived." Removing these markers needs a way to root a raw
port address across an allocation, which is a design change to the object model,
not the closing of that ticket.
